[# Section 0 — Quickstart. Front-matter; appears before the §1 intro.
   Authored 2026-05-22 against the v0.8.0-dev branch.
   Goal: a developer who has never seen CX is running CX in under five
   minutes. Cross-links use [[anchor]] form; spec references use
   ordinary markdown links. File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=quickstart n=0 title='Quickstart — first 5 minutes with CX'

  [intro
    """This is the shortest path from "I have never seen CX" to "I have
       parsed, hashed, queried, and validated my first document, and
       called CX from my host language." Eight steps, five minutes if
       your toolchain is warm. If you only have time for one section,
       read this one — then jump to [[next-steps]]."""]

  [child id=install n=0.1 title='Install'
    [intro
      """CX ships as a single statically-linked binary `cx` plus a
         shared library `libcx` for host-language bindings. Pick one
         install path:"""]

    [body
      """**Homebrew (macOS, Linux).** The fastest route on a developer
         laptop:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """brew install cx-lang/cx/cx
         cx --version"""]

    [body
      """**Manual download.** Prebuilt binaries are published with each
         tagged release on GitHub. Download the archive for your
         platform, extract, and put `cx` on your `PATH`:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """curl -L https://github.com/cx-lang/cx/releases/download/v0.8.0/cx-darwin-arm64.tar.gz | tar xz
         sudo mv cx /usr/local/bin/
         cx --version"""]

    [body
      """**Build from source.** The reference build uses devbox to pin
         the V toolchain and Python version. From a fresh clone of the
         repository:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """devbox run -- make build
         devbox run -- make install   [# installs to ~/.local/bin     #]
         cx --version"""]

    [body
      """**Docker.** No host install required; useful for CI or quick
         experimentation:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """docker run --rm -i ghcr.io/cx-lang/cx:0.8.0 cx --version
         echo '[hello name="world"]' | docker run --rm -i ghcr.io/cx-lang/cx:0.8.0 cx eval"""]

    [note
      """If `cx --version` prints `cx 0.8.0 (gNNNNNNN)` you are ready.
         If you see `command not found`, your `PATH` does not include
         the install location — see [[faq]] for recovery steps."""]]

  [child id=hello-cx n=0.2 title='Hello, CX'
    [intro
      """Every interactive CX session starts the same way: pipe a CX
         literal into `cx eval` and see what comes out."""]

    [example lang=shell
      """echo '[hello greeting="world"]' | cx eval"""]

    [body
      """The output is the same shape you typed:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[hello greeting='world']"""]

    [body
      """Three things to notice. **First**, the value evaluated to
         itself — data and code share one syntax, and a bare data
         element is its own value (see [[homoiconicity]]). **Second**,
         the canonical form normalises double quotes to single
         quotes; attribute order is preserved at parse time but
         re-sorted lexicographically for hashing (see [[canonical]]).
         **Third**, no closing tag duplication — every CX element is a
         single bracketed form. That is the whole syntax."""]

    [body
      """Try a tiny program too. The `[?for]` directive iterates and
         yields:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """echo '[?for $x :in (1, 2, 3) :yield [n $x]]' | cx eval"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """([n 1], [n 2], [n 3])"""]]

  [child id=first-file n=0.3 title='Your first file'
    [intro
      """Real CX work lives in files. Create `users.cx` with three
         users:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[users
           [user id=1 email='ada@example.com'    active=true]
           [user id=2 email='grace@example.com'  active=false]
           [user id=3 email='linus@example.com'  active=true]]"""]

    [body
      """**Parse it** (round-trips through the canonicalizer; useful
         as a syntax check):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx fmt users.cx              [# pretty-print, lossless       #]
         cx canon users.cx            [# strict canonical bytes        #]"""]

    [body
      """**Hash it** — the SHA-256 of the strict canonical bytes is
         the document's identity (see [[hash]]):"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx hash users.cx
         [# → 7f3a9b...c4e2  (64 hex chars)                            #]"""]

    [body
      """The hash is stable across machines, OS versions, and
         bindings. Two documents with the same hash are
         byte-identical in canonical form."""]]

  [child id=first-conversion n=0.4 title='Convert JSON ↔ CX'
    [intro
      """CX speaks JSON (and YAML, TOML, XML, Markdown) as
         projections — every projection round-trips. Start from
         JSON:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cat > users.json <<'EOF'
         {"users":[
           {"id":1,"email":"ada@example.com","active":true},
           {"id":2,"email":"grace@example.com","active":false}
         ]}
         EOF
         cx convert --to cx users.json > users.cx"""]

    [body
      """`users.cx` now contains the CX projection of that JSON.
         Convert back and verify the round-trip:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx convert --to json users.cx > users.roundtrip.json
         diff <(jq -S . users.json) <(jq -S . users.roundtrip.json)
         [# → no output: byte-identical after canonicalization        #]"""]

    [body
      """The hash is also stable through the round-trip — the CX form
         of a JSON document and the round-tripped JSON form share the
         same content hash, because hashing is computed on canonical
         bytes (see [[canon-cross-format]])."""]]

  [child id=first-selection n=0.5 title='Select with CXPath'
    [intro
      """CXPath is the universal selector — for reading, for
         iteration, for update focus. Same vocabulary everywhere. The
         simplest query asks: "which users are active?""""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx eval --xpath '//user[@active=true]/@email' users.cx"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """('ada@example.com', 'linus@example.com')"""]

    [body
      """Read that as: "from anywhere in the document (`//`), find a
         `user` element with `@active=true`, then take its `@email`
         attribute." If you have written XPath, the alignment is
         deliberate — see
         [`spec/cxpath_alignment.md`](../../spec/cxpath_alignment.md)
         for what carries over and what does not."""]

    [body
      """The same path drives iteration in a program:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[?for $u :in //user[@active=true] :yield
           [active-user email=$u@email]]"""]]

  [child id=first-validation n=0.6 title='Validate with a schema'
    [intro
      """A CX schema (`.cxs`) is a CX document that describes the
         allowed shape of another CX document. Write
         `users.cxs`:"""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[schema target='users'
         [element name='users' mode=strict
           [child name='user' min=1 max='*'
             [attr name='id'     type=int  required=true]
             [attr name='email'  type=str  required=true]
             [attr name='active' type=bool required=true]]]]"""]

    [body
      """Run the validator:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx validate --schema users.cxs users.cx
         [# → users.cx: ok (3 user elements validated)                 #]"""]

    [body
      """Break it on purpose to see a diagnostic. Remove `active`
         from one user and re-run:"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """cx validate --schema users.cxs users.cx
         [# → users.cx:2:3 CXER0301: missing required attribute 'active'  #]"""]

    [body
      """Schema modes are `open` / `strict` / `closed` — strict
         rejects unknown attributes, closed also rejects unknown
         child elements. See [[schema]] for the full constraint
         vocabulary."""]]

  [child id=first-binding n=0.7 title='Hello from your host language'
    [intro
      """CX ships four Tier-1 host-language bindings — V (the native
         reference), Python, Go, and Rust. The Layer-1 method set is
         identical across all four (see [[layer-1]]). One install,
         one shape:"""]

    [body
      """**Python:**"""]

    [example lang=shell
      """pip install cxlib"""]

    [example lang=python
      """import cxlib

         doc = cxlib.parse_file('users.cx')
         print(doc.hash())                       [# stable SHA-256    #]
         actives = doc.eval('//user[@active=true]/@email')
         for email in actives:
             print(email)"""]

    [body
      """**V** (native — `cxlib` is `import cx`):"""]

    [example lang=v
      """import cx
         doc := cx.parse_file('users.cx') or { panic(err) }
         println(doc.hash())
         println(doc.eval('//user[@active=true]/@email'))"""]

    [body
      """**Go:**"""]

    [example lang=go
      """import "github.com/cx-lang/cx-go"
         doc, _ := cx.ParseFile("users.cx")
         fmt.Println(doc.Hash())
         res, _ := doc.Eval("//user[@active=true]/@email")
         fmt.Println(res)"""]

    [body
      """**Rust:**"""]

    [example lang=rust
      """use cxlib::Doc;
         let doc = Doc::parse_file("users.cx")?;
         println!("{}", doc.hash());
         let res = doc.eval("//user[@active=true]/@email")?;
         println!("{}", res);"""]

    [note
      """All four bindings call into the same compiled `libcx`; hash
         values, eval results, and canonical bytes are bit-identical
         across them. That is enforced by gate 28.6 in
         [`spec/governance.md`](../../spec/governance.md)."""]]

  [child id=next-steps n=0.8 title='Where to go next'
    [intro
      """You now have CX installed, you can write data, hash it,
         convert it, query it, validate it, and call it from your
         host language. From here, follow your interest:"""]

    [list
      [item """**Learn the data language end-to-end.** [[intro]]
              walks the design philosophy; [[data]] is the
              full reference for scalars, collections, sigils, and
              mixed content."""]
      [item """**Learn the code language.** [[code]] covers the 40
              directives, CXPath in depth, patterns, builtins,
              services / workers / async, and resilience."""]
      [item """**Wire a binding into a real project.** [[bindings]]
              has per-language quickstarts and the Layer-1 / Layer-2
              method set."""]
      [item """**Pick a tooling integration.** [[tooling]] covers
              the LSP, tree-sitter grammar, VS Code, Neovim, Helix,
              `cx lint`, `cx diff`, and the playground."""]
      [item """**Migrate an existing codebase.** [[migration]]
              covers v0.7.x → v0.8.0 (automated by
              `scripts/migrate_v07_to_v08.py`) and intake from
              JSON / YAML / TOML / XML."""]
      [item """**Compare against alternatives.** [[comparison]]
              is the side-by-side against JSON, YAML, TOML, XML,
              Protobuf, MessagePack, Pkl, Dhall, Parquet, and
              Arrow — including where CX is the wrong choice."""]
      [item """**Look up a term.** [[glossary]] defines every
              CX-specific term used in this guide; [[faq]] covers
              the first-week-of-CX questions."""]]

    [note
      """The community lives at https://github.com/cx-lang/cx — file
         bugs, browse RFCs, and read the spec under `spec/*.md`. The
         playground at https://cx-lang.org/playground lets you try
         CX from a browser with no install."""]]]
